    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">How Groundwater Enters a French Drain</h2>
    <p>
      The belief that holes should face UP comes from an intuitive but false mental model: people imagine raindrops falling through the lawn and dropping into the top of the pipe like water into an open roof gutter.
    </p>
    <p>
      In reality, soil acts like a sponge. Rainwater saturates the topsoil and percolates downward by gravity until it reaches an impermeable layer (like clay, hardpan, or bedrock). Water then begins to pool and back up, creating a rising <strong>groundwater water table that rises from the BOTTOM UP</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">The 4-Inch Water Table Penalty of Holes Facing UP</h2>
    <p>
      Consider what happens when you install a 4-inch perforated pipe with the holes facing UP:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>As the water table rises through the gravel trench, water fills the bottom 1 inch of the trench. The pipe does not drain.</li>
      <li>Water fills the bottom 2 inches. The pipe does not drain.</li>
      <li>Water fills the bottom 3 inches. The pipe does not drain.</li>
      <li>Only when the water level rises <strong>4 full inches above the trench floor</strong> to reach the top holes can water finally spill over the rim into the pipe!</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      By turning the holes UP, you force the bottom 4 inches of your yard and foundation to remain permanently submerged in stagnant water before the drain pipe even begins to work.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">Why Holes Facing DOWN Drain Water Instantly</h2>
    <p>
      Standard perforated SDR-35 PVC pipe has two parallel rows of 1/2-inch holes drilled along its length. When installed properly, the pipe is rotated so the two rows of holes sit at roughly the <strong>4 o'clock and 8 o'clock positions</strong>:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-5 border border-gray-200 my-4 not-prose">
      <div class="font-bold text-gray-900 text-sm mb-2">The Hydraulic Path:</div>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-0">
        You bed the trench with 2 inches of clean washed #57 stone. When rising groundwater touches the bottom of the pipe, water takes the path of least mechanical resistance. Water flows up through the 2 inches of gravel, enters the bottom holes, and drops into the smooth interior pipe channel. Gravity immediately sweeps the water down the 1% slope toward the daylight discharge. The water table is kept permanently at the lowest possible elevation!
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Jobsite Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    author="David Miller, PE"
    role="Structural & Civil Engineering Consultant"
    experience="20+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-blue-100 text-blue-800"
    initials="DM"
    title="Why Fine Silt Enters Top Holes but Skips Bottom Holes"
    note="Another critical civil engineering reason to face holes down is sediment mechanics. When heavy rain washes through the top layer of sod, it carries microscopic sand and fine silt particles downward with gravity. If your holes face UP, that descending silt falls directly into the top holes, settling inside the pipe and forming an immovable mud bed along the bottom of the tube! When holes face DOWN, gravity forces sediment to fall harmlessly past the sides of the pipe into the bottom stone bed. Rising groundwater entering the bottom holes has already been filtered through 2 inches of crushed aggregate, entering the pipe virtually silt-free."
  />

