        title: "Laying the Pipe Directly on Soil Without a 2\" Gravel Bed",
        costImpact: "$1,600 (Bottom Holes Choked by Native Mud)",
        description: "Placing the perforated pipe directly on the dirt trench bottom presses the bottom holes directly into mud, blocking water entry.",
        solution: "Always shovel a 2-inch leveling bed of clean washed #57 stone across the trench floor before placing the pipe."
      },
      {
        number: 2,
        title: "Using Fully Perforated 360-Degree Slotted Corrugated Tubing",
        costImpact: "$2,200 (Water Leaking Back Out of Pipe on Long Runs)",
        description: "Tubing with slots around all 360 degrees allows collected water to leak straight back out the bottom holes whenever the trench passes through a dry section of yard.",
        solution: "Use rigid PVC pipe with a smooth solid unperforated bottom pan (holes strictly at 4 and 8 o'clock); the solid bottom acts as a sealed trough that carries water away."
      },
      {
        number: 3,
        title: "Using a Pipe Fabric Sock in Fine Clay Soils",
        costImpact: "$1,200 (Fabric Blinding in 18 Months)",
        description: "Clay soil particles stick to the tight fabric sock wrapped directly on the pipe, creating an impermeable crust.",
        solution: "Discard the pipe sock; wrap the exterior of the whole gravel trench with non-woven needle-punched geotextile fabric."
      }
    ]}
  />

